/**
 * Stable, machine-readable error codes for the failures a CALLER can act on.
 *
 * The problem this replaces: every failure was a bare `new Error(prose)`, so
 * the only way to tell one apart from another was to match its wording.
 * `@celo/buy-mcp` really did this —
 *
 *   msg.includes('requires Self.xyz verification')
 *
 * — which means the prose in `client.ts` was load-bearing across a package
 * boundary. Rewording an error message, or adding a detail to it, silently
 * broke an agent's ability to recognise the one failure it knows how to fix.
 * CLAUDE.md had to carry "keep this string stable" as a rule, which is a sign
 * the contract was in the wrong place.
 *
 * A `code` moves that contract into something explicit: the message stays free
 * to improve, and the code is what anyone branches on.
 *
 * WHAT BELONGS HERE. Only failures a caller can DO something about — top up a
 * balance, run `buy verify serve`, raise `--max-amount`, pick another token.
 * Internal invariants and programmer errors stay plain `Error`s; giving them
 * codes would imply a recovery path that does not exist.
 */
/**
 * The code set. Stable across releases: a code may be ADDED, but an existing
 * one must not change meaning or disappear, because agents branch on these.
 *
 * Keep this a union of literals rather than an enum — consumers can then
 * exhaustively switch on it, and it survives the package boundary as data.
 */
export type BuyErrorCode = 
/** The 402 body was not a parseable x402 envelope. */
'invalid_envelope'
/** No entry in `accepts[]` matched the configured network. */
 | 'no_matching_requirements'
/**
 * The caller expected to pay (set a `maxAmount`) and the endpoint did not
 * challenge: it answered with an error status or a web page instead of a
 * 402. Typically a dead or suspended host serving an HTML notice on every
 * route. `details.status` and `details.contentType` carry what came back;
 * the body was still passed through. Not recoverable by retrying.
 */
 | 'unexpected_response'
/** The challenge names a token that cannot carry an EIP-3009 authorization. */
 | 'token_not_signable'
/** The challenge costs more than the caller's `maxAmount` ceiling. */
 | 'amount_exceeds_max'
/**
 * The caller's `--max-amount` reads as an implausibly large DECIMAL amount
 * (1000 or more of the token) and was not confirmed with `--force`. The
 * usual cause is a units mistake: the 402 quote is atomic, the flag is not,
 * so a copied quote figure authorizes thousands of times the intended cap.
 * Refused before any request is sent.
 */
 | 'max_amount_implausible'
/**
 * The paying wallet holds less of the challenge's asset than the challenge
 * asks for, established by reading the balance BEFORE signing. Recoverable:
 * top the wallet up (`details.remedyUrl` is the faucet, on a testnet) and
 * retry. Nothing was signed and nothing was sent.
 */
 | 'insufficient_balance'
/**
 * The endpoint additionally requires a Self.xyz attestation and none was
 * found for its scope. Recoverable: obtain one, then retry.
 */
 | 'self_attestation_required'
/**
 * The required Self endpoint is valid, but this client cannot host it.
 *
 * NO LONGER EMITTED. A pinned off-chain endpoint means the server hosts the
 * receiver, and since celo-org/cpay#63 the client can verify against it — so
 * that case now yields `self_attestation_required` carrying a
 * `buy verify hosted` remedy instead of a dead end. Retained because the
 * README documents these codes as a stable contract, and a consumer still
 * branching on it should keep compiling.
 */
 | 'unsupported_self_endpoint'
/** The challenge's scheme is not one this client can sign. */
 | 'unsupported_scheme'
/** The challenge omitted the EIP-712 domain metadata signing requires. */
 | 'missing_domain_metadata'
/** The account's daily spend cap would be exceeded by this payment. */
 | 'spend_cap_exceeded'
/** No account is configured — `buy setup` has not been run. */
 | 'no_account'
/**
 * The selected signer backend could not produce a usable key for the
 * account: none stored, malformed, not matching the account's metadata, or
 * refused by that backend (the file backend refuses mainnet). `details`
 * carries which backend and why.
 */
 | 'signer_backend_unavailable'
/**
 * A plain transfer (`buy send`) was refused BEFORE anything was broadcast:
 * the node would not estimate it, or the wallet cannot pay the gas, or the
 * key would not sign. Nothing left this machine, so a retry cannot
 * double-send. `details.stage` is 'prepare' or 'sign'; `details.reason`
 * is 'insufficient_gas' when the wallet holds too little to pay the fee
 * (in CELO, and — on mainnet — in the token itself), 'simulation_failed'
 * for any other estimation failure, or 'signing_failed'.
 */
 | 'transfer_rejected'
/**
 * The signed transfer was HANDED TO THE NODE and the call then threw. It
 * may or may not have been mined; `details.txHash` is the hash to check
 * before doing anything else, and `details.doNotRetry` is true because a
 * blind retry can send the money twice.
 */
 | 'transfer_ambiguous';
/**
 * An error carrying a stable `code`, plus optional structured `details` for
 * whatever the caller needs to act (the scope to verify, the amount that was
 * refused, the command that would fix it).
 *
 * `details` is deliberately loose. It is diagnostic context for a human or an
 * agent, not a typed API — pinning a shape per code would turn every new field
 * into a breaking change, which is the trap the prose matching was already in.
 */
export declare class BuyError extends Error {
    readonly code: BuyErrorCode;
    readonly details: Record<string, unknown> | undefined;
    constructor(code: BuyErrorCode, message: string, details?: Record<string, unknown>);
}
/**
 * True when `err` is a BuyError carrying a code.
 *
 * Deliberately NOT `instanceof`. A monorepo can end up with two copies of this
 * module — a workspace build and an installed one, or two versions during an
 * upgrade — and `instanceof` silently returns false across that boundary,
 * which is precisely the kind of failure that only shows up after publishing.
 * Duck-typing on the shape survives it.
 */
export declare function isBuyError(err: unknown): err is BuyError;
/** The code for `err` when it carries one, else undefined. */
export declare function errorCodeOf(err: unknown): BuyErrorCode | undefined;
